digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper is motivated to develop and evaluate EXPERT, a prime-editing platform using an upstream sgRNA-induced cis nick and an extended or stabilized pegRNA, to test whether editable range can be expanded to upstream and downstream regions while improving efficiency and limiting drawbacks of existing PE systems.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When current prime-editing systems have unmet limitations in editable range, upstream editing, DSB risk, and efficiency, and a proposed design plausibly addresses those limitations, the design is a justified target for development and evaluation."];
  "R1" [label="(0,0,0) Because degradation of the pegRNA 3′ region can impede prime editing and structured RNA motifs at the 3′ terminus enhance pegRNA stability, engineering a stabilized or structured pegRNA 3′ extension is a plausible route to improve PE efficiency."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) A PE design that combines an upstream sgRNA-induced cis nick with a stabilized or extended pegRNA 3′ extension is a plausible design hypothesis for extending the editable window while incorporating pegRNA-stability support."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The need to expand editing beyond the narrow downstream window, including upstream of the nick, motivates testing a cis-upstream nick together with a stabilized or extended pegRNA 3′ extension as a bidirectional PE strategy."];
  "n1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, the initial prime editing (PE) system is a fusion of SpyCas9 nickase (H840A) and engineered M-MLV reverse transcriptase."];
  "n13" [label="(5,7,0) If degradation of the pegRNA 3′ region impedes prime editing efficiency, then stabilizing/structuring the 3′ extension should increase PE efficiency."];
  "n14" [label="(5,12,0) Currently, structured RNA motifs at the 3′ terminus enhance pegRNA stability (prevent 3′-extension degradation)."];
  "n2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, prime editing requires a pegRNA that includes a protospacer, an sgRNA scaffold, and a 3′ extension encoding the desired edit."];
  "n20" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, in two-pegRNA systems the two nicks are on complementary strands (in trans), which is associated with elevated probability of inducing DSBs."];
  "n21" [label="(23,9,0) If appropriately offset paired nicks on opposite strands occur simultaneously, then a double-strand break can be produced (paired nicking can yield DSBs)."];
  "n22" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given two-pegRNA in-trans nicking is linked to higher DSB probability and paired offset nicks can generate DSBs, therefore the in-trans paired-nick configuration plausibly explains the elevated DSB risk."];
  "n23" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, in two-pegRNA systems the editable region lies between the downstream directions of the two nicks."];
  "n24" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, two-pegRNA approaches still cannot edit the upstream region of either pegRNA nick."];
  "n25" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given canonical PE cannot edit upstream of the pegRNA nick and has a narrow effective range (~10 bp downstream), and two-pegRNA expands range but constrains edits between nicks and adds DSB risk, therefore there is a general unmet need for a PE strategy that enables editing on both sides of a pegRNA nick (including upstream) while maintaining low DSB/indel/off-target."];
  "n26" [label="(0,0,0) If we create a cis nick upstream of the pegRNA nick (using an upstream sgRNA) and also stabilize/extend the pegRNA 3′ extension, then the editing window may extend to both sides of the pegRNA nick (including upstream) while avoiding in-trans paired-nick DSB risk."];
  "n27" [label="(8,0,0) Currently, there is a need for expanded editable region particularly for >10 bp downstream and upstream from the nick."];
  "n3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, the pegRNA 3′ extension contains (i) a PBS complementary to the DNA protospacer segment and (ii) an RTT encoding the desired edit plus homologous sequences."];
  "n32" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: Given PE editing efficiency still needs improvement, therefore improving PE efficiency is an additional motivation/requirement for developing improved PE strategies."];
  "n33" [label="(0,0,0) Currently, developing an improved PE strategy should address both editing-range limitations and efficiency limitations."];
  "n4" [label="(4,0,0) Currently, prime editors are promising tools in modern biotechnology (so improving them is valuable)."];
  "n5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, existing PEs can modify only the downstream region of the pegRNA nick and cannot modify the upstream region, which limits editing range."];
  "n6" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, many PEs reach >10% efficiency in human cells only within ~10 bp downstream of the nick (range is narrow even when efficient)."];
  "n7" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, PE editing efficiency still needs improvement."];
  "n8" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, there is a need to expand the editable region, especially >10 bp downstream and also upstream of the nick."];
  "n20" -> "n22" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n21" -> "n22" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "n5" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n6" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n24" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n22" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n23" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n8" -> "n25" [label="induction-common"];
  "n1" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n2" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n3" -> "n25" [label="induction-case"];
  "n7" -> "n32" [label="induction-case"];
  "n4" -> "n32" [label="induction-common"];
  "n25" -> "n33" [label="induction-case"];
  "n32" -> "n33" [label="induction-common"];
  "n13" -> "R1" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n14" -> "R1" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n27" -> "R5" [label="abduction-phenomenon"];
  "n26" -> "R5" [label="abduction-knowledge"];
  "n26" -> "R10" [label="induction-case"];
  "R1" -> "R10" [label="induction-common"];
  "R10" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "R5" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "n33" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-case"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" -> "NROOT" [label="induction-common"];
}
